WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.255 --> 00:00:01.350
STEPHANIE WONG:
Hey, everyone, I've

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.210
just opened up my
awesome new hipster store

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.280
called Conventional Obscurity.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.140
I've set up my website
and all my services

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.310
are running in Kubernetes.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:10.190
But I don't know how
to manage my traffic.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.314
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Oh, I've
got a solution for you.

00:00:12.314 --> 00:00:14.327
But you've probably
never heard of it.

00:00:14.327 --> 00:00:15.910
STEPHANIE WONG: OK,
what is it called?

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.130
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
It's called Istio,

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:18.900
and it's a service
match for Kubernetes

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.660
that lets you set up networking
between your services.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.010
STEPHANIE WONG: Sounds
like that's pretty useful.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.240
Can you show me how it works?

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:24.960
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Sure thing.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.050
Let's jump into a demo.

00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:36.440
So let's first familiarize
ourselves with Istio.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.600
An Istio service
mesh is logically

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.720
split into a data plane
and a control plane.

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.870
The data plane is made up of
intelligent proxies called

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:48.790
on y's that are deployed as
side cars with micro services.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:52.820
The control plane manages
the proxies to route traffic,

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.010
and it is made up
of three components.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.800
A mixer is a platform
independent component,

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.250
and it enforces access
control and usage policies

00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.050
across the service mesh.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.209
A pilot provides
service discovery

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:09.020
for on y proxies and side
cars, traffic management,

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.310
and resiliency.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:14.030
A citadel provides the security
components, service to service,

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:15.960
and end user authentication.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.500
Let's look at this with
a real world example.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.630
We have our hipster
app, which comprises

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.570
of about 11 to 12
micro services that are

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:25.540
made in different languages.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.210
Now here's our hipster store
website built with Kubernetes.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.980
Let's add a few
items to the cart.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.050
A vintage typewriter,
and I like this camera.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:47.160
Let's add them to the
cart and place our order.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:50.160
We've completed our order
with the shipping and tracking

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.530
confirmation IDs.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.400
Now let's see what this looks
like into the Google Cloud

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.050
Platform console.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:02.380
I have a single cluster
running in Kubernetes.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.000
It's a fairly small cluster.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.039
Here I have some
workloads, as you can see.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:12.250
In the cart service,
there's a checkout service,

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.140
and then as you
scroll down, you also

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:18.310
see Istio independent
services as well

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:21.120
that are deployed there.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.610
And if you go into
one of the services,

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.230
we're going to click
on checkout service.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:32.380
You can see the CPU, memory,
and disk utilizations

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:35.950
and we're going to click
into one of the managed

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.530
pods for the checkout
service, and you'll

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:44.050
see as you scroll down that
there is an Istio proxy that's

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:48.250
installed within there,
and what that means is this

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.200
is a transparent side
car that's running

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.390
co-located within
that cart service.

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.120
These are all the services
that were deployed.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.450
Now here at this point,
you can see the end points,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.910
and where you can access
these services with their IP

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:04.660
address and the ports.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:05.550
And that's it.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.750
To summarize, we saw
an overview of Istio,

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:11.400
a live Kubernetes cluster
with multiple microservices,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.637
and Istio side cars installed.

00:03:13.637 --> 00:03:15.720
STEPHANIE WONG: Now I can
see why Istio is so hot.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.800
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Just by using Istio,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.230
you can manage your
Kubernetes services easily

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:20.820
and make your website cool.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.070
Don't forget to like,
comment, and subscribe

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.410
for our next episode on Istio.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.910
STEPHANIE WONG: And if you'd
like to get in contact with us,

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.451
click on the link
in the description.

00:03:28.451 --> 00:03:30.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

